{
  "requirement": "The review prompt MUST give a report template with these sections:\n\n- the revised text of every requirement in the group, including new split requirements, as complete directives ready to paste;\n- the revised text of requirements outside the group that the changes affect;\n- the mechanical changes, including new full names and added examples;\n- the design questions.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement says the review prompt MUST give a report template with four sections. That is a structural obligation on the prompt's content, and the supplied root `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md` carries it. In that file, `section[Requirement group review]/section[Report]` has a fenced `text` block (`block@fae2e2eda340`) that defines the template. The four sections are:\n- `### Revised text`: every requirement in the group, as complete directives ready to paste, including new split requirements.\n- `### Edits outside the group`: the revised text of other requirements the changes make stale, or \"None.\".\n- `### Mechanical changes`: one line each, listing new full names and added examples.\n- `### Design questions`: a numbered list of questions.\n\nThe paragraph after the template (`paragraph@cb926df5daf1`) adds guidance about reports with only wording changes and about writing \"No design questions.\" This does not conflict with the template. The prompt file is the only implementation root this requirement needs, and its template matches the clauses.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement is a single obligation: the review prompt must give a report template with four sections. These are the revised text of every requirement in the group (including new split requirements, as complete directives ready to paste), the revised text of affected requirements outside the group, the mechanical changes (new full names and added examples), and the design questions.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_gives_a_report_template]` is marked `{test}`. It takes the `## Report` section of the real review prompt. `section` includes the `###` subsections, because it stops only at a heading of the same or a higher level. It then uses `assert_text_in_order`, which fails if a phrase is missing or out of order.\n\nThe asserted phrases map to the clauses as follows:\n- `### Revised text`, `every requirement in the group` and `as complete directives ready to paste, including new split requirements` cover the first bullet.\n- `### Edits outside the group` and `revised text of other requirements your changes make stale` cover the second bullet.\n- `### Mechanical changes` and `list new full names and added examples` cover the third bullet.\n- `### Design questions` covers the fourth bullet.\n\nThe assertions check the sections and their described content, and they check that they appear in the stated order. A prompt that dropped a section or omitted a described content item would fail. I found no untested obligation.",
